FORMAT 1
SCENARIO History and time travel
LABEL    Worksheet 2
SUMMARY  git diff, git log, git restore and git revert on a cookbook with two commits.
INTRO    The **cookbook** repository already has two commits and is open in VS Code. The graph on the right shows the history.

SETUP
  PERSON jana NAME "Jana Nováková" EMAIL "novakovaj@jirovcovka.net" LOGIN "jana-novakova" LABEL "Jana's computer"
  MACHINE jana CONFIGURED
  REPOSITORY git/cookbook
  PS> mkdir git
  PS> cd git
  PS> mkdir cookbook
  PS> cd cookbook
  PS> git init
  FILE pancakes.md
    # Pancakes

    ## Ingredients

    - 250 ml milk
    - 1 egg
    - 120 g plain flour
    - a pinch of salt
  END
  PS> git add pancakes.md
  PS> git commit -m "Add pancake recipe"
  FILE pancakes.md
    # Pancakes

    ## Ingredients

    - 250 ml milk
    - 1 egg
    - 120 g plain flour
    - a pinch of salt

    ## Tip

    I fill them with strawberry jam.
  END
  PS> git commit -am "Add filling tip"
  VSCODE git/cookbook
END

TASK Add a method to **pancakes.md** as a numbered list (`1.`, `2.`…), save it and run `git diff` **before committing**. Then commit.
  HINT     ## Method
  HINT
  HINT     1. Mix everything in a bowl.
  HINT     2. Let the batter rest for **15 minutes**.
  HINT     3. Fry in a hot pan.
  HINT
  HINT     git diff
  HINT     git add pancakes.md
  HINT     git commit -m "Add method"
  QUESTION How are the added lines marked in the output?
  CHECK    file pancakes.md in HEAD contains line starting with "1."
  CHECK    output of "git diff" contains line starting with "+"
END

TASK Create **drop-scones.md** with headings, bullet points, a numbered list and a note in *italics* (`*text*`). Commit it with the message **Add drop scones**.
  HINT  git add drop-scones.md
  HINT  git commit -m "Add drop scones"
  CHECK file drop-scones.md in HEAD exists
  CHECK commit "drop scones" exists
END

TASK Create **README.md** with links to both recipes and commit it. Try opening a link in the preview.
  HINT  # My cookbook
  HINT
  HINT  Recipes I can cook.
  HINT
  HINT  - [Pancakes](pancakes.md)
  HINT  - [Drop scones](drop-scones.md)
  CHECK file README.md in HEAD contains "](pancakes.md)"
  CHECK file README.md in HEAD contains "](drop-scones.md)"
END

TASK Look at the history in both ways.
  HINT     git log
  HINT     git log --oneline
  QUESTION Why does your neighbour have different hashes even though they did the same as you?
  CHECK    command "git log" was run
  CHECK    command "git log --oneline" was run
END

TASK In **pancakes.md**, delete all the ingredients and save. Look at `git diff` and rescue the recipe.
  HINT  git diff
  HINT  git restore pancakes.md
  CHECK command "git restore pancakes.md" was run
  CHECK file pancakes.md contains "milk"
END

TASK Bring back **pancakes.md** as it was in the first commit, then return to the latest version.
  NOTE  You will find the hash of the first commit in `git log --oneline`.
  HINT  git restore --source=HASH pancakes.md
  HINT  git restore pancakes.md
  CHECK command "git restore pancakes.md" was run after command "git restore --source..."
END

TASK Add the line `- 2 kg salt` to **drop-scones.md**, commit it as **Add mistake** and then revert the commit.
  HINT     git commit -am "Add mistake"
  HINT     git log --oneline
  HINT     git revert --no-edit HASH
  QUESTION Did the commit “Add mistake” disappear from the history? Look at the graph.
  CHECK    commit "mistake" is reverted
  CHECK    file drop-scones.md in HEAD does not contain "2 kg salt"
END

TASK Create a file **notes-for-me.md**, then **.gitignore** with the line `notes-for-me.md`. Compare `git status` before and after. Finally commit **.gitignore**.
  HINT     git status
  HINT     git add .gitignore
  HINT     git commit -m "Add .gitignore"
  QUESTION What is the difference between git restore and git revert?
  CHECK    file .gitignore in HEAD contains "notes-for-me.md"
  CHECK    file notes-for-me.md in HEAD does not exist
END
